<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!-- The Mac App Store build's entitlements.

     It is the direct download's file with ONE key removed, and the removal is
     the reason this file exists at all:

       com.apple.security.temporary-exception.mach-lookup.global-name
         $(PRODUCT_BUNDLE_IDENTIFIER)-spks
         $(PRODUCT_BUNDLE_IDENTIFIER)-spki

     Those two Mach service names are Sparkle's channel to its out-of-sandbox
     installer. A temporary exception is exactly what its name says — a hole in
     the sandbox — and App Store review does not grant one for a second update
     mechanism, because the App Store IS this build's update mechanism. The
     `RelayiumAppStore` target does not link Sparkle, does not compile the pane
     that drives it, and therefore must not claim the exception its absence makes
     pointless. `StoreKitLinkageTests` checks both halves: no Sparkle in the
     target's link list, and no exception in this file.

     The Info.plist beside it drops Sparkle's three keys (`SUFeedURL`,
     `SUPublicEDKey`, `SUEnableInstallerLauncherService`) for the same reason: a
     feed URL is a promise to download and run code Apple did not review.

     Everything else is IDENTICAL to the direct build's, deliberately. The
     sandbox, the two network entitlements, the two file entitlements, the
     associated domain, the keychain group and the App Group are what the product
     needs to work, not what a distribution channel needs, and a Mac App Store
     build that quietly did less would be a different product wearing the same
     name. Each is argued in `Relayium/Relayium.entitlements`; the reasoning is
     not repeated here, so there is one place to correct it.

     Two of them are worth naming as App Store review surface rather than as
     mechanism:

       - `network.server` is what makes WebRTC work under the sandbox at all.
         ICE binds UDP sockets and must receive the peer's connectivity checks,
         which arrive unsolicited; without it every transfer falls back to a
         relay. It is a normal sandbox entitlement, not an exception.
       - `com.apple.security.application-groups` is the Share extension's
         hand-off container, and the extension embedded in THIS build
         (`RelayiumShareAppStore`) claims the same identifier. Its value must
         stay byte-identical to `AppGroup.macOSIdentifier`; a mismatch is the
         failure that looks like everything working, where the sheet stages a
         draft into one container and the app lists an empty one. -->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>com.apple.security.app-sandbox</key>
	<true/>
	<key>com.apple.security.network.client</key>
	<true/>
	<key>com.apple.security.network.server</key>
	<true/>
	<key>com.apple.security.files.user-selected.read-write</key>
	<true/>
	<key>com.apple.security.files.downloads.read-write</key>
	<true/>
	<key>com.apple.developer.associated-domains</key>
	<array>
		<string>applinks:relayium.com</string>
	</array>
	<key>keychain-access-groups</key>
	<array>
		<string>$(AppIdentifierPrefix)com.relayium.shared</string>
	</array>
	<key>com.apple.security.application-groups</key>
	<array>
		<string>7PVYUG4YQS.com.relayium.shared</string>
	</array>
</dict>
</plist>
